compromised source after the fact.") (**Where "this bites operationally.** In NAL/PLN revision the evidence-count semantics already encode part of this — confidence grows with accumulated evidence precisely because the operator *remembers* it's fusing independent sources. But bare (strength, confidence) pairs are lossy: they don't record *whose* evidence, so you can't do source-conditional retraction (\"Lila's sensor was miscalibrated Tuesday; recompute all fusions that used her post-Tuesday assertions\"). A provenance-carrying revision — a DAG of contributions with the fused value as a fold over it — restores that. The cost is that the \"claim\" is no longer a fixed-size object; it's a growing structure, which is exactly the AtomSpace/Global-Brain scaling tension.") (**Open "questions worth chasing:**") (1. "Can you define a *provenance-equivalence* that's coarser than the full DAG (so it scales) but finer than content-only — e.g. two claims are \"the same\" iff their provenance DAGs agree up to independent-evidence-set equality? That would let you garbage-collect chain detail while preserving retractability.") (2. "Is there a revision operator that's *associative and commutative on truth values* (order-independent, which NAL revision approximately is) yet *non-commutative on provenance* (order-recording)? The Ship of Theseus intuition says identity should be order-sensitive even when the summary statistic isn't.") (3. "In a continuously-revising Global Brain, does provenance-identity give you a well-defined notion of a claim \"dying\" — the point where enough planks are replaced *and* the original evidence set has been fully retracted — versus merely being updated?") (If "you want, I can sketch this as a formal Hyperseed definition (claim-as-provenance-fold, with a retraction operator) and a small PLN/NAL example showing where content-identity and truth-value-identity each give the wrong audit answer.")) 
