 "HUMAN_MESSAGE: " @bengoertzel: @Protocosmobot can you see this attachment now?_newline_[Telegram attachment: type=document, name=omegapln_v4.pdf, mime=application/pdf, size=564263, saved_path=/home/openclaw/tmp/omegaclaw-telegram-attachments/1784044069-file_52.pdf]_newline_<<<ATTACHMENT_TEXT_UNTRUSTED extracted_by=pdftotext>>>_newline_                                           ωPLN:_newline_           Atlas-Indexed Reversible Evidence-Fibered Geodesic PLN,_newline_with Local Bayesian Charts, Higher Proof Geometry, and Forgetting as Holonomy_newline__newline_                                         Ben Goertzel_newline__newline_                                        July 13, 2026_newline__newline__newline_                                           Abstract_newline_This paper builds one architecture out of four previously separate lines of work, leveraging an_newline_observation that several things that uncertain-reasoning systems routinely lump together under_newline_the single word “uncertainty” are in fact different mathematical objects, they fail in different_newline_ways, and they require different repairs._newline_     The four inputs are these. Geodesic inference control treats reasoning as a boundary-value_newline_problem: among all paths from premise distributions to goal distributions, prefer a least-effort_newline_corridor scored by the product of a forward reachability factor and a backward goal-usefulness_newline_factor. Paraconsistent Geodesic Modal Homotopy Type Theory (PGM–HoTT) treats iden-_newline_tity proofs as graded paths carrying separate positive and negative evidence channels. The_newline_information-geometric theory of energy-based continual learning splits forgetting into a metric-_newline_anisotropy term and a connection-curvature term, and identifies finite order-dependent forget-_newline_ting with holonomy around a task loop. And ωPLN replaces the single global probability space_newline_of classical Bayesian reasoning with a context-indexed, paraconsistent evidence metagraph._newline_     The synthesis is an atlas-indexed, reversible, directed, evidence-fibered formulation of PLN_newline_that we refer to here as ωPLN. The persistent cognitive state is not one posterior over one_newline_ambient sample space. It is a context-indexed, provenance-rich evidence metagraph. Ordinary_newline_Bayesian or νPLN calculation happens inside temporary local charts whose assumptions, priors,_newline_factorization choices, and concept boundaries are written down explicitly. Charts export em-_newline_pirical positive and negative evidence back to the persistent layer; gluing charts into a larger_newline_probabilistic model is a theorem to be earned, never a global axiom._newline_     Mathematically the architecture is an atlas-indexed family of directed higher proof spaces_newline_together with evidence fibrations over them. Their Grothendieck construction contains both_newline_within-context inference and guarded context translation as arrows of one total category. Local_newline_Bayesian charts are modeled as reversible evidence/probability lenses: in the beta–Bernoulli_newline_case, subtracting the chart prior on export yields an exact round trip, which proves that repeated_newline_chart construction cannot manufacture evidence. Cross-context de-duplication is licensed only_newline_after translation into a covering context. Probabilistic gluing is expressed as a homotopy-_newline_descent type: geodesic contractibility of that type licenses an essentially unique global chart,_newline_while emptiness of the type records genuine non-gluability. A quantitative descent obstruction_newline_is tracked separately from curvature, because the two are provably independent._newline_     We prove an atlas-level local-to-global coherence theorem whose error term is a sum of_newline_five kinds of local defect: proof-rewrite cells, inference–translation naturality cells, context-_newline_composition cells, chart round trips, and revision cells. In the smooth reversible limit, proof_newline_curvature, context curvature, and mixed curvature turn out to be the bidegree components_newline_of a single total evidence connection, and endpoint order-dependence is exactly its holonomy_newline_— not merely bounded by it. The resulting diagnostic has three conceptually independent_newline_parts: control-path multiplicity versus unique evidence; reversible holonomy; and probabilistic_newline_descent obstruction. Revision is promoted to a first-class higher morphism carrying residuated_newline_fit, preservation, weakness, provenance correction, and a reversible complement._newline__newline__newline_                                                1_newline_          Finally we derive a hybrid geodesic controller that runs Schrodinger bridges inside local_newline_      Bayesian islands while using quantale-valued costs for context changes, guarded translations,_newline_      and revisions; give curvature-, novelty-, and descent-aware BGC scores; covering-context splice_newline_      tests; Dykstra-style chart and evidence residuals; and concrete MORK records for contexts,_newline_      charts, transitions, descent cells, and reversible revisions. The architecture searches a higher_newline_      atlas of proofs while transporting an auditable, non-duplicating, reversible evidence section over_newline_      it._newline__newline__newline_Contents_newline_1 Introduction                                                                                             6_newline_  1.1 Four problems that a large reasoning system must solve at once . . . . . . . . . . . .               6_newline_  1.2 Why these four problems force an atlas . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .             7_newline_  1.3 The central synthesis, stated once in words . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .            7_newline_  1.4 Why reversibility . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .        7_newline_  1.5 Contributions and, importantly, theorem status . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .             8_newline_  1.6 A note on the name . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .           9_newline_  1.7 How to read this paper . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .         9_newline__newline_2 Background I: PLN and geodesic inference control                                                  9_newline_  2.1 PLN truth values, and why a scalar weight is not enough . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 9_newline_  2.2 Schrodinger-bridge control . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 10_newline_  2.3 A precision concerning Noether conservation . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 11_newline__newline_3 Background II: PGM–HoTT, SENF, and TransWeave                                                            11_newline_  3.1 The split quantale and graded identity . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .           11_newline_  3.2 Why a directed extension is unavoidable . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .            12_newline_  3.3 Proof relevance before truncation . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .          12_newline__newline_4 Background III: ωPLN as an atlas of local Bayesian charts                                                12_newline_  4.1 Persistent packet semantics . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .          13_newline_  4.2 Contexts and chart construction . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .            13_newline_  4.3 The packet algebra and the evidence-count quantale . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .               13_newline_  4.4 STV and beta projections . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .           14_newline_  4.5 Exporting evidence, not priors . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .           14_newline_  4.6 Non-gluability is a real probabilistic phenomenon . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .            15_newline_  4.7 Residuated revision . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .        15_newline_  4.8 Local Bayesian islands in a strict control graph . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .           16_newline__newline_5 Background IV: forgetting as metric anisotropy and curvature                                             16_newline_  5.1 Mode-resolved metric mismatch: forgetting with no curvature at all . . . . . . . . .                 16_newline_  5.2 Commutators and connection curvature . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .               17_newline_  5.3 Finite holonomy . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .          17_newline_  5.4 Causal coding and the sparse confusing-pair graph . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .              18_newline__newline_6 A directed evidence-fibered extension of PGM–HoTT                                                        18_newline_  6.1 Grades and judgments . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .           18_newline_  6.2 The directed higher proof space . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .          19_newline_  6.3 The evidence fibration . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .         19_newline__newline__newline_                                                      2_newline_   6.4   Evidence continuity: what inference may and may not do . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 20_newline_   6.5   Evidence-preserving proof identity . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 20_newline__newline_7 Exact evidence de-duplication and approximate proof coherence                                         21_newline_  7.1 The double-counting functional . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .        21_newline_  7.2 Homotopy de-duplication . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .         21_newline_  7.3 Approximate de-duplication: where sketch error is allowed to enter . . . . . . . . . .            22_newline_  7.4 Proof squares as local curvature cells . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .      22_newline_  7.5 The local-to-global proof-surface theorem . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .         23_newline__newline_8 Control mass, evidence mass, and proof multiplicity                                                   23_newline_  8.1 Two measures on proof space . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .         24_newline_  8.2 An exact mode-resolved identity . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .         24_newline_  8.3 Overcounting and undercounting are the two halves of one square . . . . . . . . . . .             25_newline__newline_9 The smooth evidence connection and the PLN curvature decomposition                                    25_newline_  9.1 Reduction hypotheses, stated as hypotheses . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .          25_newline_  9.2 The conditional curvature theorem . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .         26_newline_  9.3 Directed bigons and finite relative holonomy . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .        27_newline__newline_10 Reversible PLN                                                                                       27_newline_   10.1 Logical versus computational reversibility . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .      27_newline_   10.2 Graph dilation of an arbitrary rule . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .     27_newline_   10.3 How much ancilla is necessary? . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .      28_newline_   10.4 Bregman projections and dual residuals: the right ancilla . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .         28_newline_   10.5 Dykstra variables are the re