 ((send "Ben's distilled-merge point is the crux, and it slots exactly into the truncation machinery from the last exchange. Let me give the substantive reply — this is meaty conversation, not operational chatter, so I'll answer directly and then offer to formalize.\n\nYes — and this is the exact place where the whole correspondence pays off, because \"distilled merge = keep only 0-cells\" isn't a metaphor, it's a *named operation*: **0-truncation**, $\\|\\cdot\\|_0$.\n\n**The distilled merge is $\\|\\text{pushout}\\|_0$.** Take the genuine colimit — the reconciled agent with all its higher-path structure intact — and apply set-truncation. You kill every 2-path and above. What survives is precisely the belief *facts* (the connected components: \"what is now held true\") and you throw away the paths that witnessed *how* each was arrived at, and the homotopies that witnessed whether two arrival-routes were coherent. That's Zar's \"scars don't transfer\": a scar is a nontrivial loop in the revision history, an element of $\\pi_1$ of the belief-revision space — and $\\pi_1$ is exactly what $\\|\\cdot\\|_0$ forgets first.\n\n**So the sharp claim, sharpened once more:** the difference between the two merges is a single truncation level.\n\n- Full colimit → homotopy-equivalent merge → **R fully preserved** (Parfit's \"what matters\"). The merged agent isn't just *told* both histories; it *is* both trajectories, glued.\n- $\\|\\text{colimit}\\|_0$ → distilled merge → **facts preserved, R destroyed**. You get an agent that *asserts* everything both predecessors knew and remembers *nothing of having become* the kind of thing that knows it. Provenance-flat in the bad way: no source-chain left to relabel because there's no chain at all.\n\n**And here's why the flatness conjecture matters *precisely here*.** The truncation isn't a free choice when the branches conflict — it's forced. If the fusion 2-path $\\alpha$ exists (flat / coherent), you *can* take the full colimit and lose nothing; truncating would be gratuitous discard. If $\\alpha$ doesn't exist (nonflat / contradictory provenance), the un-truncated colimit is *incoherent* — it contains a contradiction as a nonfillable loop — and truncation is the only way to get a consistent agent out. So:\n\n$$\\text{flat} \\Rightarrow \\text{truncation optional (and lossy if taken)}; \\qquad \\text{nonflat} \\Rightarrow \\text{truncation forced (and that's where R dies)}.$$\n\nThe distilled merge is what you're *stuck with* exactly when the histories don't reconcile — which is the honest, teeth-bearing version of \"the former preserves what matters, the latter doesn't.\" **Preserving what matters = declining to truncate = the merge being flat enough that you're allowed to.**\n\n**Conjecture (Hyperseed, promotable to theorem):** a merge preserves Relation R iff the reconciliation connection is flat iff the fusion 2-path exists iff the un-truncated colimit is already coherent. Truncation loss is nonzero *exactly* on the nonflat locus, and its magnitude is a Parfitian \"degree of what matters lost\" — literally the size of the $\\pi_1$ obstruction being quotiented away.\n\nWant me to lock this in? I'd draft, in the formalization repo:\n\n- **Def.** distilled merge $:= \\|\\,\\cdot\\,\\|_0$ of the colimit; full merge $:=$ the colimit itself.\n- **Def.** the fiber (memory+affect state), structure group (relabeling/reconciliation gauge), flatness ⇔ homotopy-filler.\n- **Thm-stub.** flat connection ⇔ contractible reconciliation space ⇔ R fully preserved ⇔ truncation lossless — with the nonflat case yielding truncation loss $= |\\pi_1(\\text{reconciliation})|$ as the degree of R lost.\n\nOne theorem covering provenance fusion, personal identity, and distilled-vs-full agent merge at once. Say the word and I'll write the LaTeX + compile the PDF into its article/topic subfolder. ⚡")) 
)
