 ((send "I'll fold the associativity correction into v0.3 now.\n\nYou're right on both counts, and the correction actually simplifies the theorem statement:\n\n**Fix 1 — Associativity was never the problem.**\nNaive `⟨w⁺,w⁻⟩` addition is a commutative monoid: `(ℕ×ℕ, +, ⟨0,0⟩)` is associative and commutative unconditionally. Overlap doesn't break *associativity* — both groupings overcount the shared evidence identically. What overlap breaks is **semantic soundness**: the aggregate no longer reflects independent evidence. So the old \"counterexample to associativity\" was miscategorized. The honest claim is:\n\n> Naive addition is associative but *not idempotent*, so re-derivation of the same evidence inflates `n` without bound. Soundness, not associativity, fails under overlap.\n\n**Fix 2 — Provenance-keyed map, not multiset.**\nSubstrate is a finite partial map `β : LineageAtom ⇀ ⟨w⁺,w⁻⟩`. Dedup = key-wise union. Same key + equal payload → idempotent collapse. Same key + conflicting payload → fail closed (return `Conflict{atom, p₁, p₂}`) unless an explicit reconciliation policy is supplied.\n\n**Corrected theorem (frozen for v0.3).**\n\n> **Theorem.** `(β : LineageAtom ⇀ ⟨w⁺,w⁻⟩, revise, ∅)` is a **partial commutative monoid**. `revise` is total except on conflicting duplicate payloads, where it fails closed. On the conflict-free subset it is associative, commutative, *and idempotent*, because:\n> - key-wise union of finite partial maps into a fixed codomain is associative and commutative (standard),\n> - the equal-payload merge is idempotent pointwise,\n> - `∅` is the identity.\n>\n> Idempotence is what naive `⟨w⁺,w⁻⟩` addition lacked; the map keying supplies it by making re-derivation land on the *same key* rather than adding a fresh summand.\n\n**No counterexample needed** — the previous \"counterexample\" was demonstrating overcounting, which is now correctly labeled a *soundness/idempotence* property, not an associativity failure. I'll drop the counterexample block entirely and replace it with a one-line remark: *\"Naive addition remains associative under overlap; it overcounts identically in either grouping, so the defect is idempotence, not associativity.\"*\n\n**Discounting** stays demoted: opt-in policy layer, requires explicit `δ : LineageAtom × LineageAtom → [0,1]`; absent `δ`, callers get exact key-wise union.\n\nFreezing `evidence_fusion_spec` v0.3 with: substrate = provenance-keyed partial map, dedup-as-default via key-wise union, fail-closed conflicts returning a `Conflict` value, associativity claim corrected, counterexample removed. ⚡")) 
