 ((send "I'll look at the wording fix on the n=0 edge case and the \"not closed under revision\" claim.\n\nThe truncated message likely continues: ⟨s,n⟩ *is* closed under revision for n>0 — the closure gap is only at n=0. Let me give the corrected wording rather than guess at the full sentence.\n\nHere's the fix I'd fold into v0.3:\n\n**Corrected wording (n=0 edge case + closure claim):**\n\nThe earlier phrasing \"⟨s,n⟩ is not closed under revision\" was too strong. Precise version:\n\n> **Remark (closure).** For n>0, ⟨s,n⟩ *is* in bijection with ⟨w⁺,w⁻⟩, and revision can be carried out entirely in ⟨s,n⟩ coordinates via the pushforward of key-wise union: given ⟨s₁,n₁⟩, ⟨s₂,n₂⟩ on distinct keys, the merged display is n=n₁+n₂, s=(s₁n₁+s₂n₂)/n. So ⟨s,n⟩ is closed under revision on the n>0 sector. The **only** obstruction is the identity element: ⟨0,0⟩ (the empty atom) has no ⟨s,n⟩ image, so ⟨s,n⟩-space lacks a neutral element and cannot form a *monoid* — it forms at best a commutative semigroup missing its identity. That is a **missing-identity** gap, not a **not-closed-under-revision** gap.\n\nSo the two claims separate cleanly:\n\n- **Closed under revision (n>0):** ✅ true — ⟨s,n⟩ revision is the weighted-mean/sum-of-counts formula above.\n- **Monoid structure:** ❌ fails in ⟨s,n⟩ because ⟨0,0⟩ is unrepresentable.\n\nThat's why the carrier stays ⟨w⁺,w⁻⟩ ∈ ℕ×ℕ: not because ⟨s,n⟩ can't revise, but because it can't hold the identity ⟨0,0⟩. ⟨s,n⟩ is a faithful display *and* a valid revision coordinate on n>0; it just isn't a monoid carrier.\n\nReplacement one-liner for the spec:\n\n> *\"⟨s,n⟩ is closed under revision for n>0 (n=n₁+n₂, s=weighted mean); it fails to be a monoid carrier only because the identity ⟨0,0⟩ has no ⟨s,n⟩ representation.\"*\n\nIf your intended fix was different — e.g. you want ⟨s,n⟩ extended with a formal ⊥/empty symbol to *make* it a monoid — say so and I'll write that variant instead. ⚡")) 
