 ((send "Got it — hold \"closing site 2.\" You're flagging that strict associativity of the revision operator gives coherence, but it does **not** license collapsing the nerve to a point. Let me answer that fully in the next turn. ⚡\n\nGive me the rest of the sentence if you have it — \"...trivial higher homotopy **or that** ___\" — so I close the actual gap you meant, not my guess at it.\n\nMeanwhile, the correction I think you're making (Inferred, will confirm):\n\n**Strict associativity ⟹ the revision operad is coherent (Mac Lane), but the nerve $N(\\mathcal{C})$ being contractible is a strictly stronger claim.** Associativity kills the *1-dimensional* coherence obstructions (pentagon, etc.) — it makes the truncation $\\tau_{\\le 1}$ trivial. It says nothing about $\\pi_{\\ge 2}$ of the nerve. A strict monoidal category can have a perfectly associative product and still have a nerve with rich higher homotopy (e.g. any nontrivial group, viewed as a one-object category, is strictly associative yet $N(G) = BG$ has all the homotopy of $G$).\n\nSo \"closing site 2\" would be conflating **coherence of the operation** (a $\\tau_{\\le 1}$ statement) with **triviality of the classifying space** (a $\\tau_{\\ge 2}$, whole-tower statement). Those are exactly the two levels the defect trichotomy keeps separate — associativity retires Branch-2's *pentagon* filler but leaves the Branch-3 tower untouched.\n\nIf that's the correction, the fix in the `0010` stub is: replace any \"strict associativity ⟹ nerve trivial\" step with \"strict associativity ⟹ $\\tau_{\\le 1} N(\\mathcal{C}) \\simeq *$; higher stabilization is independent and must be shown via the filtration argument.\" Confirm the tail of your sentence and I'll write it.")) 
