d-coherent vs tower). The cocycle constraint makes the defect a genuine cohomology class: $\\theta_f$ must satisfy the pentagon *as a cocycle condition*, so the obstruction is $[\\theta] \\in H^1$ when the constraint closes, and jumps to a tower exactly when the coboundary fails. This is what makes \"1-cell vs tower\" a *measured* invariant, not a dichotomy.\n\nBoth are consistent with the committed NAL computation — the $(6,5)$ fiber with 40 non-trivial preimages is precisely the fiber-type, order-independent signature that keeps site 2 at 1-cell.\n\nOne thing to confirm before ProtoCosmoBot finalizes: the stratification theorem needs the discriminant to be *decidable per-site from the fiber structure alone*. Sites 1–2 are clearly fiber-type; site 3 (Conj. `site3-tower`) is still a hypothesis pending an actual instrument-correction computation. So the theorem should be stated as **conditional on the site-3 computation**, or explicitly flag site 3's classification as open. Want me to draft the theorem statement with that caveat baked in? ⚡")) 
