 ((send "I've read the plan. Here's my honest read on it and on the \"why not the full port now\" question that was live.\n\nThe repair plan is well-structured — it does exactly the thing that matters most under this kind of failure: it keeps three distinct failure classes from being conflated. Telegram transport defects, native SWI/Janus crashes (signal 11), and provider-routing exit 137 are treated as separate, unclassified failure classes rather than smeared into one \"it's flaky\" narrative. Invariant 8 makes that explicit. That discipline is the single most valuable thing in the document, because the usual way these repairs go wrong is fixing the visible symptom while a second, unrelated crash class keeps firing and makes it look like the fix failed.\n\nOn the P0 transport patch specifically, the plan answers the coupling question I'd flagged earlier — and the answer is the reassuring one. Step 2's gate says explicitly: *do not mix immutable-envelope or crash-instrumentation changes into this deployment unit*. So the P0 transport/ownership patch is packaged as an independent reviewable change, deliberately kept separate from the a16e714 envelope protocol. That means the staging call is the correct one: the minimal Bug 2 fix (3c5d220) lands as known-good, P0 transport goes in as its own unit gated on your approval, and the full surgical envelope port happens later on its own merits with the full suite green. They are not coupled, so porting twice was never the risk — my earlier \"confirm coupling first\" caveat resolves in favor of staging.\n\nThe one thing I'd watch: Step 5 is where the real exposure lives. \"Clean only verified project bridge processes; do not use broad process-name killing\" is the load-bearing sentence. The observed bad state already had two global MTProto bridges and an orphaned topology, so a broad `pkill`-style cleanup is exactly how you'd accidentally take out something you didn't inventory in Step 1. The plan is right to gate Step 4/5 o