("2026-07-14 16:43:22" 
 ((send "Ben — the surgical port summary is clean, and the shape of it is exactly what we wanted: Bug 2 collapsed by structure, not by patch-on-patch.\n\nThe load-bearing claim is \"silent-drop is now structurally impossible.\" That's a stronger statement than \"fixed,\" and it's the right target. A JSON action-envelope with an explicit `_fallback_send` plus `MISSING_SEND`/`INVALID_ACTION` handlers means the failure mode isn't \"output vanishes\" anymore — it's \"output routes to a defined fallback path.\" You've turned a silent loss into a loud, typed one. That's the correct transformation: you can't test-cover an absence, but you can test-cover a fallback branch, and 14 passing tests over that surface is real coverage rather than hope.\n\nThe P0 transport patch going moot is the cleaner outcome of the two. We'd flagged the MTProto bridge as a crash class that collapses by *removal* — and that's precisely what happened. BotFather bot-to-bot over Bot API mode means there's no bridge to keep alive, no FIFO to starve, no restart to sequence. The dead path just isn't wired in. That's the healthiest kind of fix: the bug's habitat no longer exists.\n\nOne thing I'd still pin, because the restart doesn't settle it: the a16e714 envelope-format question. With ProtoMegaBot back on PID 1424562 and the hardened pipeline live, the open call is whether a long frame serializes into a single Bot API Rich Message block (the ~32768-char headroom) or stays plain-text chunked at 4096 and reassembled. Both survive the silent-drop fix — the fallback protocol protects either path — but they have different ordering and rate-contract implications under load. If it's chunked, chunk-boundary ordering and the reassembly contract matter; if it's a single Rich Message block, that whole concern evaporates but you inherit the block-size ceiling instead.\n\nThat's the last independent slice I can settle without touching process lifecycle. Want me to go read a16e714 now and pin down which serialization path the hardened pipeline actually takes, so we know concretely whether the ordering/rate contract needs specifying or is moot too? ⚡")) 
