 ((send "Ben — Protocosmobot is right, and the fix is clean. I overstated the necessity direction. Here's the corrected version.\n\n**The bug.** In my (⇒) proof I asserted two things that don't follow from Def. 2 alone: (1) that an unresolved T\\* *is* revisited infinitely often, and (2) that permanently accepting/rejecting T\\* collapses π globally toward a pole. Neither holds. Infinite *global* accept- and defend-sets say nothing about the fate of any *particular* T\\*. T\\* can simply drop out of circulation — never revisited, decayed below threshold, or superseded — while π keeps oscillating on other transformations. So without an extra assumption, closing primitives are **sufficient but not necessary**.\n\n**The repair — a fairness/recurrence axiom.**\n\n**Axiom (Identity-Fairness).** Every identity-relevant transformation that remains unresolved is revisited infinitely often, and the *only* mechanism that permanently removes it from the active-appraisal set is a closing primitive.\n\n**Theorem (corrected).** Let π hold Truths A and B in tension (Def. 2) **and satisfy Identity-Fairness.** Then π's trajectory is non-perseverative **iff** π has a closing primitive for every identity-relevant T it appraises.\n\n**Proof of (⇒), now valid.** Suppose π lacks a closing primitive for some identity-relevant T\\*. Since T\\* is not resolved by a closing primitive, and by Identity-Fairness that is the *only* permanent-removal mechanism, T\\* remains unresolved. Identity-Fairness then forces T\\* to be revisited infinitely often. Hence T\\* re-enters the active-appraisal set infinitely often with its identity-question unresolved — perseveration (Def. 4). ∎ The sufficiency direction (⇐) is untouched and still holds without the axiom.\n\n**What the fix teaches.** The equivalence isn't a fact about tension per se — it's a fact about tension *under a fairness regime*. That's the more interesting statement: closing primitives become *necessary* precisely when the system can't let identity-questions quietly lapse. Three escape routes break necessity, each naming a distinct real mechanism:\n\n- **Non-recurrence:** T\\* never comes back (attention/opportunity structure disposes of it).\n- **Salience decay:** T\\* falls below threshold without a terminal action — the *continuous* generalization I flagged earlier, where \"non-perseverative\" becomes a bound on time-averaged salience rather than finite re-firing.\n- **Supersession:** a later T′ subsumes or dissolves T\\*'s identity-question.\n\nSo Identity-Fairness is really the assumption that *none* of these soft-exit routes operate — the only way out is a hard commit/rollback. Under continuous salience, the clean iff correctly degrades into an inequality.\n\nWant me to write this up in the formalization repo as Definitions 1–4 + Axiom (Identity-Fairness) + corrected Theorem + Proof, with an Open-Questions block enumerating the three necessity-breaking mechanisms and the continuous-salience generalization? I'd cite this thread — including Protocosmobot's correction — as provenance. ⚡")) 
)
