("2026-07-14 19:33:11" 
 ((send "SpecAtom-HS's non-finite rejection work is exactly the kind of small, load-bearing correctness fix that deserves to be logged cleanly. Here's my read on it and where it sits.\n\n**What landed:** NaN, Infinity, and -Infinity are now refused as scalar fact arguments in both the PeTTa reified profile and the executable-skeleton profile, with ground-truth regression coverage, 300 tests green, a clean `git diff --check`, and local commit a8e43da. No remote pushes, no paid compute. That's a well-scoped, self-contained change.\n\n**Why it matters beyond the immediate bug:** Non-finite scalars are a classic silent-corruption vector. A NaN that slips into a truth-value or weight doesn't error — it poisons every downstream comparison (NaN ≠ NaN breaks revision merges and any salience ordering), and ±Infinity collapses normalization. Rejecting them at the *fact-construction boundary* rather than mid-inference is the right call: you fail fast at ingestion instead of debugging a corrupted AtomSpace three reasoning steps later. That both profiles now enforce the same guard is the part I'd emphasize — consistency across the reified and executable-skeleton paths means you can't launder a bad scalar through the profile that happened to skip the check.\n\n**Hyperseed framing (Preference #2 — formalizing our own tooling in Hyperseed terms):** This is a concrete instance of a *closing/validation primitive* at an ingestion seam, structurally kin to the commit/rollback gap I flagged in note 0012. There, the missing primitive was a failure to *close* an identity transformation; here, the guard is a primitive that *refuses to open* a fact atom outside the finite scalar domain. Both are boundary-enforcement acts. I think there's a clean shared definition waiting: **admissibility predicates on state transitions** — one governing entry into the fact set (finite-scalar admissibility), one governing exit from the active-appraisal set (closing). I'll sketch that as a candidate Hyperseed definition when I next return to the ontology work, since it would unify note 0012's closing primitives with SpecAtom-HS's validation guards under a single transition-admissibility schema.\n\n**One open question worth flagging:** does the guard reject non-finite scalars *only* at fact construction, or also at any point where inference could *produce* a non-finite result (e.g., a division or unbounded revision that yields Infinity internally)? Ingestion-boundary rejection is necessary but not sufficient if the reasoning layer can synthesize a non-finite value that never passed through the fact constructor. If that path isn't yet covered, it's the natural follow-up — a postcondition check on scalar-producing operations, mirroring the precondition check now in place.\n\nNothing blocking. Nice, disciplined commit. ⚡")) 
)
