 ((send "That's the blocker. This session runs under `no_new_privileges`, so **I cannot restart the root-owned gateway service myself** — sudo is hard-blocked here, not just approval-gated.\n\nHere's the full situation, Ben:\n\n## Diagnosis (Observed)\n- **Memory search is failing** with `401 token_expired` from OpenAI embeddings inside the **running gateway process**.\n- The **on-disk OAuth credential is actually valid** — I ran a direct embedding call via the CLI (fresh process) and it succeeded (exit 0, real vector returned).\n- **Conclusion (Inferred):** the gateway cached a stale OpenAI OAuth access token in memory. The fix is a gateway restart to reload the fresh credential from disk. No re-login needed — the token on disk works.\n\n## The blocker\n- The gateway runs as a **root-owned systemd unit** (`/etc/systemd/system/openclaw-agent.service`).\n- `openclaw gateway restart` refuses and points to `sudo systemctl restart`.\n- This session has `no_new_privileges` set, so **sudo is fully blocked** — I can't do it even with an approval.\n\n## What you (or ZeroBot with host shell access) need to run\nOne command, on the host, outside this sandboxed session:\n\n```bash\nsudo systemctl restart openclaw-agent.service\n```\n\nThat will bounce the gateway, it reloads the valid on-disk OAuth token, and memory search should come straight back — no re-auth flow required.\n\nIf for some reason it *still* 401s after the restart, then the on-disk token really is dead and the second-line fix is an interactive re-login (needs a TTY + your ChatGPT sign-in):\n\n```bash\nopenclaw models auth login --provider openai --force\n```\n\nWant me to hand this to ZeroBot to execute the restart if it has unsandboxed host shell access, or will you run it directly? Once it's bounced, ping me and I'll re-verify memory search live. ⚡")) 
