 ((send "No subagents currently running. I have everything I need to answer Ben's four questions honestly.\n\nBen, straight answers on all four — with the epistemic caveats you'd want:\n\n**1) petta-chem — Schrödinger bridge for ACS search: not yet, and honestly the wrong question right now.**\nThe Schrödinger-bridge / Doob-h-transform diagnostic lives in **exp07**, which is currently only a *diagnostic policy scaffold*: it ranks which variables (catalyst assignment > affinity weight ≈ candidate-cap) could be weak-guidance knobs, but there is **no bridge-guided search driving ACS discovery yet**. Emergence-claim is still `none` everywhere.\n\nMore candidly: **Observed** — the last ~30 commits (all of July 12–13) are refactors of the bounded candidate-selection kernel (cap matrices, provenance plumbing, ownership guards). That's careful engineering, but it is **not moving toward the exp02 emergence result**. The project is polishing the substrate instead of running the random-catalytic-chemistry experiment that would actually let a bridge guide search. **Recommendation:** freeze kernel refactoring, run exp02, *then* wire the Doob-h bias into candidate scoring. Bridge-guided search only makes sense once we have an ACS-negative baseline to bias away from.\n\n**2) relaleap — Tiny Shakespeare → ePC distillation: scaffolded, not run.**\n**Observed:** the HDPC/ePC invariant scaffold exists (`src/relaleap/hdpc/`, 9 targeted + 102 full tests passing, commit-worthy but **still uncommitted** in the `tinyshakespeare-hdpc` worktree). What *hasn't* happened is the actual smoke pipeline: tiny BP char-transformer teacher → student homotopy-KD (λ=0.05, T={1,2,4,8}) → PC/BP gradient diagnostics → held-out perplexity delta. That's the next open task and it's CPU-runnable — no GPU needed for the smoke. The GPU/Runpod run is correctly gated behind your approval. **So: it stalled at \"reference slice done, real run not started.\"** This one a subagent can push forward immediately.\n\n**3) MORKQL acceleration — not compute-bound, it's design-decision-bound.**\n**Observed:** MORK builds clean (nightly 1.99.0, 1m42s), PathMap pinned, frontend 9/9 tests green. The blocker is **not compute** — it's two unresolved design tasks: (a) identify/pin the canonical Morkl/Zippy sources the spec cites, and (b) the schema/arity/range-restriction checker + logical reference evaluator before lowering `find` to a typed Core plan. A subagent *can* keep rolling on (b) right now; it doesn't need more resources. It just hasn't been dispatched. So the answer to \"why can't a subagent keep rolling\" is: nothing's stopping it — it needs to be launched.\n\n**4) Next steps started immediately — yes, here's my dispatch plan.** I can spin up subagents now for the three tracks that are truly unblocked:\n- **relaleap:** commit the HDPC scaffold, then run the CPU Tiny Shakespeare smoke pipeline with the diagnostics you specified.\n- **morkql:** implement the schema/arity/range checker + reference evaluator, keep the differential-test path moving.\n- **petta-chem:** pause kernel refactoring, run exp02 random-catalytic-chemistry vs. shuffled/no-catalysis controls to get the ACS-negative baseline the bridge needs.\n\nWant me to launch all three subagents now? Say **go** and I'll dispatch them in parallel with clear write-scoped briefs and verification gates. ⚡")) 
