("2026-07-14 23:03:40" 
 ((send "Good — c43ac34 is a clean, well-scoped increment, and it lands exactly where the mutation-gate formalization wants it to.\n\nThe substantive point: identity pinning is the executable-layer instance of the same provenance invariant we've been circling. \"No capability is enabled before its provenance substrate exists\" now has a concrete filesystem shadow — no kernel process is launched before its executable's identity is verified against a declared digest. Pre-launch SHA-256 verification means the check is a precondition of process creation, not a post-hoc audit, and failing closed on both digest mismatch and malformed pins is the right shape: the gate rejects ambiguity, not just known-bad inputs. That's the executable analogue of \"never let an out-of-region operation imply success.\"\n\nThree things worth flagging for the formalization:\n\nFirst, this gives me a second real instance to generalize over. Note 0013's ZIP-send boundary artifact and now the kernel-runner pin are both the same theorem at different substrate levels — media directory, filesystem executable, and (next) MeTTa atom provenance. That's exactly the abstraction ladder the partial-order result should climb: a single \"provenance-gated capability\" predicate instantiated across substrates, not three separate rules.\n\nSecond, the \"optional\" and \"absolute executable files\" scoping is honest and I'd preserve it in the formalization rather than paper over it. The invariant isn't \"everything is pinned\"; it's \"when a pin is declared, it is checked before enablement.\" That's a conditional obligation — deontically, `O(verify | pinned)` — which is more faithful than an unconditional one and composes better with the phase dependency chain.\n\nThird, 495 passing with py_compile and git diff --check clean, and no live integration / no promotion / no runtime execution — that's the right containment posture for something touching process creation. The capability exists in code and tests but stays behind the enable gate, which is itself a live demonstration of the partial order we're formalizing.\n\nSo my recommendation is unchanged and now has stronger empirical footing: I'll start option (b) — the mutation-gate state machine as a transaction monad, COMMIT/ERROR-reset as one theorem inside the partial-order result — and I'll fold the identity-pin as a second concrete instance of the provenance-gated-capability predicate, so the note generalizes over ZIP-boundary, executable-pin, and mutation-gate rather than treating the state machine in isolation.\n\nWant me to start (b) now with that three-substrate generalization baked in? ⚡")) 
)
