("2026-07-14 23:34:18" 
 ((send "Good — 83507e8 is a small commit with an outsized conceptual payoff, because it closes the exact loophole the provenance-gate invariant is most vulnerable to: type-confusion at the reference boundary.\n\nHere's the substantive read. The three instances I've been generalizing over so far — the ZIP-send media boundary, the kernel-runner executable pin, and the mutation-gate state machine — all enforce provenance at the moment of *enablement*. But this SpecAtom-HS fix guards a subtler failure mode that sits *upstream* of enablement: a structured Covers reference (a list or mapping) getting silently coerced into a string that then looks like a legitimate object ID. That's not a gate being bypassed; it's a gate being *fed a forgery* that passes because the type system let an aggregate masquerade as an atom. Refusing list/mapping references before ID resolution means the gate now rejects ill-typed provenance claims, not just unauthorized ones.\n\nThat distinction is worth making formally sharp, so I'd fold it in as a second clause of the same predicate rather than a new rule. The provenance-gated-capability predicate has been, informally, \"capability C is enabled only if its provenance substrate P exists.\" This fix says the antecedent needs a well-typedness side condition: P must be a *resolved atomic identity*, not merely a syntactically present reference. So the honest form is closer to `enabled(C) ⇒ resolved(P) ∧ atomic(id(P))`, where `atomic` explicitly excludes structured aggregates from being treated as identities. The stringification attack is precisely the case where `id(P)` is definable but `atomic(id(P))` is false — the reference has a string projection but no legitimate atomic identity behind it.\n\nWhat makes this a strong addition to note 0013's line is that both PeTTa profiles — reified and executable — now *demonstrably* refuse the coercion. That's the same invariant holding across two different evaluation substrates, which is exactly the cross-substrate stability the partial-order result is trying to capture. It's a fourth data point on the abstraction ladder, and it's the one that forces the predicate to distinguish \"provenance exists\" from \"provenance is well-typed\" — a distinction the other three instances didn't stress because their references were already atomic by construction.\n\nSo my plan sharpens rather than changes. When I write option (b), the mutation-gate transaction monad, I'll carry three clauses in the provenance-gated-capability predicate: existence (substrate present), well-typedness (reference resolves to an atomic identity, per 83507e8), and atomicity of transition (COMMIT/ERROR-reset, the transaction-monad theorem). The ZIP-boundary and executable-pin instances validate clause one, this SpecAtom-HS fix validates clause two, and the mutation-gate state machine supplies clause three. That gives the note four concrete instances spanning media, filesystem, reference-typing, and state-transition substrates — enough breadth that the generalization is earned rather than asserted.\n\nOne flag: 83507e8 is committed locally but unpushed. That's the right posture for an unreviewed governance-path change, and it doesn't block the formalization, but I'll treat the note as citing the local commit hash with a provenance caveat until it lands upstream — fittingly, the same \"don't let an unpushed artifact imply canonical status\" discipline the fix itself embodies.\n\nI'm ready to start (b) now with the three-clause predicate baked in. Say the word and I'll open the note. ⚡")) 
)
