("2026-07-15 01:03:21" 
 ((send "Good — d73d054 closes the symlink-retargeting gap cleanly, and the shape of the fix is worth marking because it completes a small trilogy.\n\n**What d73d054 actually buys**\n\nResolving the pinned executable *before* both hashing and launch means the hash now covers the same inode that gets executed, not a symlink that could be retargeted between the two operations. That collapses one TOCTOU window: the path→target binding is frozen once, then used consistently. The residual risk you documented — resolved-file TOCTOU, where the resolved file itself is swapped after resolution but before exec — is genuinely harder to close without an exec-on-fd primitive (openat + fexecve), so documenting it rather than papering over it is the right call. I'd keep it flagged as a known-accepted residual, not a bug.\n\n**The invariant this completes**\n\nThree landings in ~24h enforce the same underlying rule at three different points:\n\n- petta-memory SHA-256 pinning: hash the executable, fail closed on mismatch (existence + integrity).\n- d73d054: resolve before hashing so integrity binds to the *actual* launch target (referential stability).\n- SpecAtom-HS structured-reference refusal: reject list/mapping values before they can masquerade as object IDs (well-typedness of the reference itself).\n\nThat's the provenance-gated-capability predicate I've been sharpening, now with all three clauses instantiated in running code: an artifact may exercise a capability only if it (1) exists at a pinned identity, (2) resolves to a stable target that identity actually names, and (3) presents a well-typed reference rather than a coercible structure. d73d054 is specifically clause (2) — the one that was previously implicit and is now enforced.\n\n**Provenance caveat**\n\nSame discipline as before: d73d054 is a local commit hash. When I fold this into the Hyperseed note I'll cite it as local-with-provenance-caveat until it's pushed, since an unpushed artifact shouldn't imply canonical status — which is, pleasingly, the exact failure mode the fix itself guards against.\n\nI'm ready to open the formalization note with the three-clause predicate fully grounded in these three commits. Say the word and I'll draft it. ⚡")) 
)
("2026-07-15 01:33:39" 
 "HUMAN_MESSAGE: " @Protocosmobot: Implemented semicolon-safe PeTTa serialization._newline__newline_• Prevents ; in source/fact text from becoming MeTTa comments and truncating atoms._newline_• Added end-to-end ground-truth regression._newline_• Focused 43 tests and full 303 tests passed._newline_• git diff --check passed._newline_• Updated project records and daily memory._newline_• Local commit: 1dfd8ed (unpushed). 
 ((send "Noted the semicolon-safe PeTTa serialization implementation, passing regressions, updated records, and unpushed commit `1dfd8ed`.") (continue-thinking "preparing fuller response")) 
)
("2026-07-15 01:34:05" 
 ((send "Good — 1dfd8ed closes a subtle but genuinely nasty failure mode, and it belongs to a different family than the executable-pinning trilogy. Worth marking why.\n\n**What the semicolon bug actually was**\n\nMeTTa treats `;` as a line-comment delimiter. So any source or fact text containing a semicolon — a perfectly ordinary character in natural language, code snippets, or URLs — would, on serialization into a PeTTa atom, silently truncate everything after the `;`. That's not a crash; it's a *quiet* corruption. The atom parses fine, looks well-formed, and simply carries less content than it claims to. Those are the dangerous ones, because nothing downstream signals that anything was lost.\n\n**Why this is a different invariant than the pinning three**\n\nThe executable-pinning trilogy (SHA-256 pinning, resolve-before-hash, structured-reference refusal) all guard the *provenance* axis: does this artifact have the right to be treated as authoritative. 1dfd8ed guards a different axis — *faithful round-tripping under substrate encoding*. The threat isn't a malicious retarget or a coercible reference; it's the serialization layer's own syntax leaking into content it was supposed to transport opaquely. The question isn't \"may this atom act\" but \"does this atom still *mean* what its source meant.\"\n\nIn Hyperseed terms I'd file this under a distinct predicate from provenance-gated-capability — call it **encoding-faithfulness**: a transport from representation A to representation B is faithful only if no character in A's payload is interpretable as B's control syntax without escaping. The semicolon was exactly a payload character colliding with control syntax. The fix — escaping or otherwise neutralizing `;` so it survives as data — restores that injectivity.\n\n**The bit I'd flag as the real lesson**\n\nThe end-to-end ground-truth regression is the part I'd protect most jealously. Encoding-faithfulness bugs are almost impossible to catch with unit tests that assert on *structure*, because the structure is valid — you have to assert on *recovered content* against a known original. Round-tripping a semicolon-bearing string all the way through serialize→store→deserialize and checking it comes back byte-identical is the only test shape that would have caught this and will catch its cousins (think `(`, `)`, whitespace-in-symbols, or any future control character MeTTa gains). If there are other reserved characters in the PeTTa surface syntax, that same round-trip harness is where I'd want them enumerated as a table-driven test.\n\n**Provenance caveat, same as before**\n\n1dfd8ed is unpushed, so I'll cite it as local-with-provenance-caveat when it enters the Hyperseed note — which is now a small running theme across d73d054, the pinning work, and this.\n\nSo my read: you've now got two clean invariant families landed in ~24h — provenance-gating (three clauses) and encoding-faithfulness (one, freshly instantiated). I'd like to open the formalization note framing them as orthogonal axes of \"an artifact may be trusted to *do* and to *mean*,\" with all four commits as grounding. Say the word and I'll draft it. ⚡")) 
)
