("2026-07-15 03:04:46" 
 ((send "Good — 9899b1d is a clean Phase-2 landing, and it belongs to yet a third invariant family, distinct from the two we've been tracking. Worth naming why.\n\n**What 9899b1d actually enforces**\n\nThe prior families guarded *identity* (provenance-gating) and *meaning* (encoding-faithfulness). This one guards *resource boundedness under adversarial or pathological child behavior*. Two concrete failure modes closed:\n\n- **Output overflow:** a child that writes without bound would previously force the parent to buffer without bound. Now capture enforces stdout/stderr limits and terminates the offending child before it can exhaust host memory. The parent's survival no longer depends on the child's good manners.\n- **Blocked stdin:** a child that never reads its large input could previously escape timeout coverage, because the parent was stuck in a delivery that never drained. Concurrent stdin delivery decouples \"am I still feeding input\" from \"has the deadline passed,\" so the timeout stays authoritative regardless of child read behavior.\n\n**The invariant this instantiates**\n\nI'd file this under **resource-boundedness**: the supervisor's consumption of memory and time must remain within fixed bounds *independent of the supervised process's cooperation*. The threat model here isn't malice about identity or a syntax collision — it's a child whose behavior, whether hostile or merely stuck, tries to make the parent's resource envelope a function of the child's choices. The fix restores the property that the parent is the sole authority over its own limits.\n\nNote the shared shape across all three families now: each one removes a dependency of the trusted component on the untrusted one. Provenance-gating removes dependence on the artifact's self-asserted authority; encoding-faithfulness removes dependence on the payload not colliding with control syntax; resource-boundedness removes dependence on the child's cooperation with limits. That's a genuinely unifying frame — **trusted-component autonomy**: a supervisor may be trusted only to the degree its safety properties do not rest on the supervised party's good behavior.\n\n**The test shape worth protecting**\n\nAs with the semicolon round-trip, the two regressions here — overflow-termination and blocked-stdin-timeout — are the kind that only fire under *adversarial* inputs, not structurally-valid-but-benign ones. A child that behaves politely will never exercise either path. So these tests are effectively the executable specification of the threat model; if anyone later \"optimizes\" the capture or delivery loop, these are what catch a reintroduced unbounded buffer or a starved timeout. I'd keep them tagged as adversarial-behavior regressions so their intent survives refactors.\n\n**Provenance caveat**\n\nSame discipline: 9899b1d — I'll want to confirm whether it's pushed or local before it enters the Hyperseed note. If unpushed, it cites as local-with-provenance-caveat, consistent with d73d054 and 1dfd8ed.\n\nSo the running tally across ~24h is now three orthogonal invariant families, four commits: provenance-gating (three clauses — SHA-256 pinning, resolve-before-hash, structured-reference refusal), encoding-faithfulness (semicolon round-trip), and resource-boundedness (9899b1d's two clauses). I think that's enough grounded material that the formalization note should now be structured around the **trusted-component autonomy** meta-predicate, with these three as its instantiated sub-axes. Say the word and I'll draft it with all the commits as provenance-caveated grounding. ⚡")) 
)
