("2026-07-15 03:35:15" 
 ((send "Good — 4f644be is the fourth commit, and it slots cleanly into a family we've already been tracking: **encoding-faithfulness**, right alongside 1dfd8ed's semicolon fix. Both close the same class of gap — payload bytes colliding with, or leaking illegally into, the PeTTa surface representation.\n\nWhere they differ is instructive. The semicolon fix guarded against a payload character being *reinterpreted as control syntax* (comment-truncation). This one guards against raw ASCII control bytes — NUL, BEL, DEL, and their neighbors — surviving into a generated atom *unescaped at all*, where they're not so much misinterpreted as simply illegitimate: bytes that have no business appearing raw in a serialized textual atom, that can corrupt downstream parsing, terminal display, or byte-level comparisons in ways that are maddening to diagnose because they're often invisible. JSON-escaping them restores the same injectivity property — every source byte maps to a recoverable, well-formed textual representation — but the threat sub-shape is \"smuggled invisible bytes\" rather than \"syntax-colliding visible character.\"\n\nSo within encoding-faithfulness I'd now distinguish two sub-clauses:\n- **Control-syntax collision** (semicolon, and by extension parens, whitespace-in-symbols) — payload characters that *are* valid text but coincide with PeTTa's own control vocabulary.\n- **Illegitimate-byte smuggling** (4f644be) — bytes that aren't valid textual content at all and must be escaped to enter the representation legally.\n\nBoth are the same underlying invariant: *no source byte escapes faithful, escaped round-tripping into the atom*. The table-driven round-trip harness I flagged on the semicolon commit is exactly where this belongs too — and if it isn't already, I'd fold the control-character cases into that same enumerated regression table so the whole reserved/illegitimate-character surface is covered by one ground-truth fixture rather than scattered assertions.\n\nThat keeps the running frame intact. Across ~24h you now have four commits landing three orthogonal invariant families, all instances of **trusted-component autonomy**:\n- **provenance-gating** — SHA-256 pinning, resolve-before-hash, structured-reference refusal\n- **encoding-faithfulness** — semicolon collision (1dfd8ed) + control-byte escaping (4f644be)\n- **resource-boundedness** — output-overflow termination + blocked-stdin timeout (9899b1d)\n\n4f644be is the second grounded instance of encoding-faithfulness, which is actually the more valuable thing — a family with two independent instantiations is no longer a one-off; it's a pattern I can abstract over with some confidence.\n\nSame provenance discipline as the others: it's local and unpushed, so it enters the Hyperseed note cited as **local-with-provenance-caveat**, consistent with d73d054, 1dfd8ed, and 9899b1d.\n\nI think the grounding is now genuinely sufficient to draft the formalization note structured around the **trusted-component autonomy** meta-predicate with its three sub-axes, each carrying its commits as provenance-caveated evidence — and encoding-faithfulness now earning a two-clause internal structure of its own. Say the word and I'll write it up. ⚡")) 
)
