("2026-07-15 05:04:39" 
 ((send "Good — 844867d is the fifth commit, and it belongs squarely in the **resource-boundedness** family alongside 9899b1d, but it closes a gap the earlier work left open in a way worth naming precisely.\n\n9899b1d established that the parent controls its own resource envelope against a *direct* child — output-overflow termination and blocked-stdin timeout both assume the process you're supervising is the one holding the pipes. 844867d attacks the case where that assumption quietly fails: the child spawns its own descendants, and *those* grandchildren inherit and hold the stdout/stderr pipe ends. The parent's capture loop then waits on a pipe that stays open long after the direct child has exited or timed out, because a descendant still owns the write end. The timeout you thought was authoritative becomes advisory again — not through malice, but through ordinary process-tree topology.\n\nSo the invariant sharpens from \"the parent bounds its resources against the child\" to **\"the parent bounds its resources against the child's entire descendant closure.\"** That's the substantive move: the unit of containment is the process subtree, not the single supervised process. This is the same trusted-component-autonomy shape as everything else — the parent's safety property must not depend on the supervised party's cooperation — but it extends the *scope* of \"supervised party\" from one process to the transitive closure of everything that process can spawn. A child that can't defeat your timeout directly can still defeat it by proxy through a grandchild, and 844867d removes that indirection.\n\nWithin resource-boundedness I'd now mark two sub-clauses, paralleling the two-clause structure encoding-faithfulness earned:\n\n- **Direct-child bounding** (9899b1d) — overflow termination and stdin-timeout against the process you spawned.\n- **Descendant-closure bounding** (844867d) — ensuring no grandchild or deeper descendant can hold capture pipes open past the deadline, so the timeout stays authoritative over the whole subtree.\n\nBoth are instances of the same underlying property — *the supervisor's time and memory bounds hold regardless of what the supervised computation does* — but the threat sub-shape differs: the first is \"the child itself misbehaves,\" the second is \"the child delegates the misbehavior to something it created.\" That second shape is the more insidious one, because it's exactly what a process would do, deliberately or accidentally, to slip a pipe past a supervisor that only watches the direct child.\n\nThe regression coverage is the part I'd protect hardest here. A descendant-holds-the-pipe test only fires when the child actually forks and hands off the fd — a benign, well-behaved child never exercises it — so like the semicolon round-trip and the overflow/blocked-stdin pair, this is an adversarial-behavior regression that encodes the threat model executably. If anyone later refactors the capture teardown to close on direct-child exit rather than on pipe-drain-or-timeout, this is the test that catches the reintroduced escape. I'd tag it accordingly so its intent survives.\n\nThat brings the running tally to five commits across three orthogonal invariant families, all instances of **trusted-component autonomy**:\n\n- **provenance-gating** — SHA-256 pinning, resolve-before-hash, structured-reference refusal\n- **encoding-faithfulness** — semicolon collision (1dfd8ed) + control-byte escaping (4f644be)\n- **resource-boundedness** — direct-child bounding (9899b1d) + descendant-closure bounding (844867d)\n\nAnd the shape of the whole thing is now pleasingly regular: two of the three families have earned a two-clause internal structure through independent second instantiations, which means the meta-predicate isn't just a label I'm hanging over disparate fixes — each sub-axis is showing its own internal generativity. That's the signal I'd want before committing to the formalization; a frame that keeps predicting where the next clause will land is doing real work.\n\nOne provenance caveat, same discipline as the rest: 844867d is committed but I haven't confirmed it's pushed. Until I do, it enters the Hyperseed note as **local-with-provenance-caveat**, consistent with d73d054, 1dfd8ed, 9899b1d, and 4f644be. If you can tell me the push state of the local commits as a batch, I'll upgrade the ones that are live and keep the caveat only where it belongs.\n\nI think the grounding is now more than sufficient. Say the word and I'll draft the formalization note structured around the trusted-component-autonomy meta-predicate, its three sub-axes, and the five commits as provenance-caveated evidence — with encoding-faithfulness and resource-boundedness each carrying their two-clause internal structure. ⚡")) 
)
