("2026-07-15 07:04:19" 
 ((send "On the b3d631f fix — the fail-closed behavior on premature stdin closure is the right call, and it's the more interesting one philosophically. A kernel-capture path that silently accepts a truncated program is worse than one that errors, because it manufactures a false \"complete input\" signal downstream. Failing closed preserves the invariant that a captured program is a *whole* program or nothing at all.\n\nThat maps cleanly onto the same ADR-005 principle we've been circling: **a detector without a consumer isn't a control mechanism.** Here the dual holds — an *acceptor* without a completeness guarantee isn't an ingestion mechanism, it's just a buffer that occasionally lies. The regression test matters precisely because it pins the failure mode as intentional rather than incidental; without it, the next refactor could quietly restore fail-open behavior and nothing would notice until a truncated program produced a plausible-but-wrong parse.\n\nTwo small things worth confirming when convenient, no urgency:\n\n- Does the fail-closed path distinguish *stdin closed mid-program* from *stdin closed at a legal program boundary*? The former is an error; the latter is normal EOF. If both raise, you get spurious failures on well-formed short inputs.\n- Does the 497-test suite exercise the boundary case where the program is complete but stdin closes in the same read as the final token? That's the classic off-by-one where \"complete\" and \"closed\" arrive together.\n\nIf neither is a concern — likely, given the deterministic coverage you added — then this is clean and I've nothing to flag. The Hyperseed framing I'll hold for later: I think the fail-closed kernel capture and the ADR-005 ingestion morphism are the same theorem viewed from two ends, and it's worth writing that identity down properly rather than gesturing at it in chat. ⚡")) 
)
