("2026-07-15 07:06:07" 
 ((send "Thanks for the full July 15 summary — it's a clean snapshot, and a few things stand out as worth acting on rather than just noting.\n\n**The genuinely urgent item is the failed recovery cron.** Everything else is a decision or a deferred priority, but a silently-failed recovery job is the kind of thing that erodes trust in the whole automation layer. If the recovery cron is what's supposed to bring workers back after a crash, its failure means the \"safety net\" is currently down while several workers are already disabled. That combination — disabled workers *plus* a broken recovery path — is the one I'd inspect first. Worth checking whether it failed transiently (env, lock contention) or structurally.\n\n**The stale PID bookkeeping on the OmegaClaw side is the second cleanup I'd prioritize.** PID file says 1437624; live workers are 1455829/1455833/1455836. That's harmless until something *reads* the PID file to decide whether to restart or kill — at which point stale bookkeeping either spawns a duplicate or signals the wrong process. It's the same fail-closed principle from the b3d631f kernel fix: a record that confidently lies is worse than an absent one. Reconciling it now is cheap; debugging a phantom double-worker later is not.\n\n**On the decisions you're being asked for**, most are yours alone (RelaLeap scientific direction, OmegaSim preregistration restart, SpecAtom-HS repo name/visibility, OmegaClaw topology approval). I won't push on those. The one I'd gently flag: the pattern of *disabled workers with unexplained reasons* — ThreadKeeper, both petta-chem crons, GGB roadmap, and two watchdogs. Before re-enabling any of them, it's worth recovering *why* each was disabled, because \"re-enable everything that's off\" is how you resurrect the exact failure that caused someone to disable it. The watchdogs especially — a disabled watchdog plus a broken recovery cron means two independent guard layers are both down.\n\n**On the research substance**: petta-memory being the only running worker and continuing the fail-closed Phase-1 episode/runtime path is the right focus, and it's consistent with the b3d631f trajectory. The petta-chem Exp07 weak-guidance diagnostics — staying off RAF/emergence overclaims is exactly correct; that's where the temptation to overinterpret is strongest.\n\nIf you want, I can pull the failed recovery-cron logs and the OmegaClaw PID reconciliation into a concrete diagnosis right now — those two are the only items where I can make progress without a decision from you. Everything else waits on your direction. ⚡")) 
)
